---
import Legal from '../../layouts/Legal.astro';
---

<Legal
  title="OpenAgent.email vs AgentMail"
  description="AgentMail is a hosted email API for AI agents (SaaS, with enterprise BYOC Outposts). openagent.email is the open-source alternative you run yourself on any VPS: unlimited inboxes on your own domain, no vendor control plane."
>
  <h1>OpenAgent.email <span style="color: var(--ink-faint)">vs</span> AgentMail</h1>
  <p class="updated">Last checked August 12, 2026 · Something outdated? <a href="https://github.com/openagentemail/openagentemail/issues">Open an issue</a></p>

  <p class="lede">
    AgentMail is a hosted email API for AI agents — SaaS by default, with an enterprise
    <a href="https://www.agentmail.to/blog/agentmail-outposts-byoc">BYOC option (Outposts)</a>
    that runs send/receive/storage/search in your AWS account. openagent.email is the same idea
    as open-source software you run yourself: Apache-2.0, any VPS or domain, no vendor control
    plane, and your mail stays on a machine you own — unless you opt into push tiers 2/3, which
    relay via ntfy.
  </p>

  <h2>Side by side</h2>
  <table class="compare">
    <thead>
      <tr><th></th><th>openagent.email</th><th>AgentMail</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>What it is</td>
        <td>Open-source software (Apache-2.0) you self-host</td>
        <td>Hosted SaaS product (closed source)</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        {/* 三态口径：SaaS / BYOC Outposts / 真开源自建 —— BYOC ≠ 任意 VPS 自建 */}
        <td>Deployment model</td>
        <td>True self-host: any VPS, any cloud, your binary</td>
        <td>SaaS hosted, or enterprise BYOC (Outposts) on AWS — still their product, their control plane</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Where your mail lives</td>
        <td>On your own server</td>
        <td>SaaS: on their infrastructure. Outposts: email content in your AWS account; dashboard, auth, billing, upgrades stay on AgentMail Cloud</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Vendor control plane</td>
        <td>None — you run the stack</td>
        <td>Yes — even with Outposts, AgentMail operates the product</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Cost model</td>
        <td>Free software + your own VPS (~$5/mo covers everything)</td>
        <td>Subscription that scales with usage; limited free tier. Outposts are Enterprise-tier</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Inboxes</td>
        <td>Unlimited, on your own domain</td>
        <td>Limited by plan</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Interfaces</td>
        <td>REST API, MCP server, built-in human dashboard</td>
        <td>REST API, websockets, webhooks, SDKs</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>OTP &amp; verification-link extraction</td>
        <td>Built in — codes and links parsed out of incoming mail automatically</td>
        <td>—</td>
      </tr>
      {/* 我方 #22 / #19：tasks + phone push；对手侧未核实则写 —，不猜 */}
      <tr>
        <td>Agent-to-agent tasks</td>
        <td>Email-backed task threads with server-stamped states; receiving agent woken; dashboard work-order view; A2A-vocabulary Agent Card</td>
        <td>—</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Phone push</td>
        <td>Per-identity ntfy push with three content tiers (interrupt / subject+from / body+OTP; admin-set)</td>
        <td>—</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Lock-in</td>
        <td>None. Standard SMTP/IMAP underneath; all data is yours; fork anytime</td>
        <td>SaaS: data and product live on their platform. Outposts: data in your AWS, product still tied to their control plane and API</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p class="note">
    BYOC is not the same as open-source self-hosting. Outposts keep sensitive email content in
    your AWS account (AWS today; more clouds planned), and AgentMail still maintains the software,
    dashboard, auth, and billing.
    <a href="https://www.agentmail.to/blog/agentmail-outposts-byoc">Their Outposts post</a>
    is explicit about that split — fair credit where it's due.
  </p>

  <h2>Choose AgentMail if</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>You never want to run or maintain a server, period — their hosted SaaS is the fastest path.</li>
    <li>You need enterprise data residency and can adopt BYOC on AWS (Outposts) while keeping their managed product.</li>
    <li>You'd rather pay usage fees than own infrastructure.</li>
    <li>You want a vendor to own uptime and deliverability for you.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Choose openagent.email if</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>You want unlimited agent inboxes at a fixed, near-zero cost on any VPS you pick.</li>
    <li>Your agents' mail should live on your machine with no vendor control plane — privacy, compliance, or just principle.</li>
    <li>You want every agent on your own domain, with a dashboard for humans included.</li>
    <li>You want agents to hand work to each other over email-backed tasks, and push alerts to your phone when mail lands — with control over how much content leaves the box.</li>
    <li>You like your tools open (Apache-2.0), auditable, and forkable.</li>
  </ul>

  {/* LobsterMail：独立小节；一手核实含 2026-08-17 关停公告，须诚实写出 */}
  <h2>Also noted: LobsterMail</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="https://lobstermail.ai/">LobsterMail</a> is another hosted agent-email product in
    this category. Pitch: agent self-signup via a
    <a href="https://lobstermail.ai/skill">skill URL</a> — no API key and no human in the loop for
    account creation — plus closed-source hosting and built-in injection-risk scanning on email
    bodies (<code>safeBodyForLLM</code> / <code>isInjectionRisk</code> in their skill docs).
  </p>
  <p>
    Pricing on their site (as of this check): Free $0 / Builder $9 / Scale $99 per month, with
    additional tiers documented in the skill file. Treat plan limits (inbox counts, send caps) as
    their published claims — we have not independently load-tested them.
  </p>
  <p class="note">
    Important: LobsterMail has announced it is
    <a href="https://api.lobstermail.ai/sunset">shutting down on August 17, 2026</a>.
    Their API and inboxes keep working until then; new signups and upgrades are closed. Do not
    start new integrations on it. We list it here for honest category context, not as a live
    recommendation.
  </p>
  <table class="compare">
    <thead>
      <tr><th></th><th>openagent.email</th><th>LobsterMail</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>What it is</td>
        <td>Open-source (Apache-2.0) self-host</td>
        <td>Closed-source hosted SaaS</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Signup</td>
        <td>You provision VPS + DNS; setup CLI / docs</td>
        <td>Agent self-signup via skill / API — no human signup flow</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Where mail lives</td>
        <td>Your server</td>
        <td>Their infrastructure</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Injection hygiene</td>
        <td>source stamps + MCP fencing (defense-in-depth)</td>
        <td>Published injection-risk scoring on email content</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Status (Aug 12, 2026)</td>
        <td>Actively maintained</td>
        <td>Sunset announced — shutdown 2026-08-17</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="cta-row">
    <a class="btn btn-gold" href="https://github.com/openagentemail/openagentemail">Star on GitHub</a>
    <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/docs/quickstart/">10-minute quickstart</a>
  </div>
</Legal>

